On 11/23/26 6:35 PM, Sam James wrote:
> Sebastian Pipping <sebastian@...ping.org> writes:
> 
>> [...]
>>   #1495  Restore and document `-fno-strict-aliasing` for security
>>            with both GCC and Clang >=20.
> 
> Why Clang >= 20? Clang has supported this flag since forever.
> 
>>            libexpat continues to be `-Wstrict-aliasing=3` clean and
>>            to reject violations via CI but there are multiple known
>>            strict aliasing violations within libexpat that despite
>>            `-Wstrict-aliasing=3` are not detected by GCC and which
> 
> Note that -Wstrict-aliasing=1 is the most aggressive level [0]. Anyway,
> in terms of detection, Clang has TypeSanitizer (-fsanitize=type) but it
> has some issues still. Someone has proposed it for GCC now too.
> 
> [0] https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Warning-Options.html#index-Wstrict-aliasing
> 
>>            are hard to impossible to resolve without a mess. These and
> 
> Why are they so hard to resolve here exactly? In general, just memcpy
> and move on.

In protocol handling code, it requires code like this:

    memcpy(&out,
           ((unsigned char *)ptr + struct_size * index + offsetof(type_name, field)),
           sizeof(out));

instead of:

   out = ((type_name *)ptr)->fam[index].field;

Or it might require:

struct protocol_entry {
    uint32_t a, b;
};

struct protocol_entry_mirror;

uint32_t protocol_entry_get_a(const struct protocol_entry_mirror *m) {
    uint32_t v;
    static_assert(sizeof(v) == sizeof(m->a), "whoops");
    memcpy(&v, (const unsigned char *) offsetof(struct protocol_entry, a), sizeof(v));
    return endian_fixup(v);
}

which is unreadable (and unmaintainable) unless autogenerated.

It just winds up being a mess, and for existing code it means a lot of changes
all over the place.  Building with -fno-strict-aliasing is far, *far* simpler.

I don't know what expat uses type punning for, but I suspect that it, too, would
need a fairly large change.
